## Why This Is Asked

New angle: multi-tenant QoS in CNPA stacks, focusing on concrete enforcement rather than generic fixes. It tests how to translate policy into concrete config, tooling, and rollback plans under load.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant QoS and isolation
- PostgreSQL per-tenant pool quotas
- Kafka producer per-tenant limits and topic sharding
- HTTP backpressure and degradation strategies
- Canary rollout and per-tenant monitoring

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo middleware: per-tenant quota enforcement
function quotaCheck(req, res, next) {
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'];
  const limit = getTenantQuota(tenant);
  if ((inFlight[tenant] || 0) >= limit) return res.status(429).send('Too busy');
  inFlight[tenant] = (inFlight[tenant] || 0) + 1;
  res.on('finish', () => { inFlight[tenant]--; });
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate rollback safety when quotas tighten?
- What dashboards and alerts surface per-tenant backlog and SLA breaches?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical cache-coherence strategies under CNPA load, covering Redis locking, API backpressure, and Kafka pacing with realistic validation.

## Key Concepts
- Cache stampede prevention
- Redis distributed locking (SETNX, EX) and TTL refresh
- Per-key rate limiting and circuit breakers
- Backpressure across HTTP, Redis, and Kafka
- Observability and staged rollout validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js sketch for cache refresh with Redis lock
const lockKey = `lock:${cacheKey}`;
if (await redis.set(lockKey, '1', 'NX', 'EX', 30)) {
  const value = await loadFromDBOrCompute(cacheKey);
  await redis.set(cacheKey, value, 'EX', 300);
  await redis.del(lockKey);
} else {
  // another worker refreshes
  await sleep(50);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor lock contention and adjust TTLs at scale?
- How would you test resilience when Redis cluster experiences partial outages?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical, end-to-end protection of PII in a CNPA pipeline with minimal friction for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- Field-level redaction policy
- Transformations across HTTP, DB, Kafka, Redis
- Validation and leak-coverage observability

## Code Example
```javascript
function redact(record, policy){
  if (policy.email) record.email = mask(record.email);
  if (policy.phone) record.phone = mask(record.phone);
  return record;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify policy changes against compliance requirements without disrupting traffic?

## Why This Is Asked

Test practical tracing and end-to-end visibility across CNPA during retries and regional outages, ensuring the correlation persists across services and layers.

## Key Concepts

- Correlation IDs / distributed tracing across HTTP, DB, Kafka, Redis
- Middleware propagation, headers, and context propagation
- Retry semantics, idempotent writes, and rollback strategies
- Observability: logs, metrics, traces, dashboards

## Code Example

```javascript
// Express middleware to ensure correlation ID propagation
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const id = req.headers['x-correlation-id'] || uuidv4();
  req.correlationId = id;
  res.setHeader('X-Correlation-Id', id);
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify the correlation ID is preserved through PostgreSQL writes and Kafka messages?
- How would you test this under retries and partial outages, including automated end-to-end tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-service atomicity and failure handling across CNPA components.

## Key Concepts
- Transactional outbox pattern
- Exactly-once semantics with Kafka transactions
- Idempotent Snowflake ingestion via dedupe keys
- End-to-end verification and monitoring

## Code Example
```sql
CREATE TABLE outbox (
  id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
  aggregate_id UUID,
  payload JSONB,
  emitted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,
  emitted_at TIMESTAMPTZ
);
```

```sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE users SET name='...' WHERE id=...;
INSERT INTO outbox (id, aggregate_id, payload) VALUES (gen_random_uuid(), ?, to_jsonb(...));
COMMIT;
```

```javascript
// worker pseudo
while (row = nextOutbox()) {
  kafkaProducer.send({topic:'users', value: row.payload, transaction: true});
  markEmitted(row.id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- What failure modes would you test (Kafka publish fail after DB commit; DB crash during txn; consumer offset commit failure)?
- How would you scale the solution to high cardinality events and ensure end-to-end latency remains within SLOs?

## Why This Is Asked
Introduces access control in a CNPA stack, a common beginner-friendly area with real-world implications.

## Key Concepts
- RBAC, middleware, JWT validation
- Log redaction and PII protection
- Unit/integration tests, basic load testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Express-style middleware sketch
function rbac(requiredRoles){
  return (req,res,next)=>{
    const token=req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];
    const payload=verifyJwt(token); // assume helper
    if(!payload || !requiredRoles.includes(payload.role)) return res.status(403).send('Forbidden');
    req.user=payload; next();
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support multi-tenant roles?
- How would you test for token revocation and short-lived tokens?
